  "pages": 1,
  "notes": [
   "Each entry lists the first pasuk of a seder: seder number, perek, pasuk. Numbering follows תנ\"ך קורן (מספור הסדרים ע\"פ תנ\"ך קורן).",
   "Parenthesized seder numbers mark boundaries found only in some ancient manuscripts; they are not part of the numbered sequence.",
   "Sefarim split into additionals (שמואל, מלכים, דברי הימים, עזרא נחמיה) print the sub-sefer name before the perek.",
   "The PDF covers Neviim and Ketuvim only - it lists no Torah sedarim. That is a source limitation, not evidence that the Torah has no seder division.",
   "Extraction: positional reconstruction of the PDF Hebrew text objects (right-to-left columns, split glyphs, merged header fragments), cross-checked entry-by-entry against the rendered page. Manual curation verified all 297 rows."
  ]
 },
